# Development environments
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo

# Claude Code session worktrees and local settings. A session creates its worktree in the
# current directory, so sometimes inside this one, and each is a full copy of the repository.
# Until this folder became a repository of its own the rule lived in the monorepo's .gitignore,
# a file this repository does not carry.
.claude/

# RimWorld converts every PNG to a .dds next to it the first time the game loads the mod. They are
# a per-machine cache, regenerated on demand. Ignoring them here does not keep them out of a folder
# uploaded straight from disk. The game wrote Mod/Textures/Things/Building/Joy/CrystalBall.dds
# beside its PNG on 2026-09-23 at 14:13, and PublishedFileId.txt appeared at 14:31: an upload from
# disk after 14:13 carries the cache file. Whether the 0.1.0 item was sent that way is not recorded.
*.dds

# Test evidence: Pickle's -EvidenceDir target. Proof kept on disk for the session that reads it, not
# something the repository carries: captures and Player.log make it grow without bound (PickleTools
# saw 10 GB in a day). What is worth keeping goes to docs/runs/ as a short text summary. TESTING.md
# says which proofs to keep.
Tests/Pickle/Evidence/
evidence/

# The Pickle step assembly is a build artefact of the test companion, rebuilt by
# `dotnet build Tests/Pickle/Source/CrystalBall.PickleSteps.csproj -c Release` and read from this
# folder when the game is staged. It stays on disk, never in git. This mod ships no assembly of its
# own, so there is no Mod/Assemblies/ to keep apart from it.
Tests/Pickle/Mod/Pickle/Assemblies/

# Build intermediates. Tests/Pickle/Source/Directory.Build.props sends them here, outside the
# companion mod folder, so that nothing of a build ever ends up in a folder the game stages.
.build/
